Invalid example programs of several files, one case per program, in byte
order of name.

A case named n is its files, each a txtar file n/<path>, followed by
n/expected, the compiler's output for it, with each file's path printed as
n/<path>. The case is built from the directory n, or from the directory an
optional n/.target file names, relative to n. A directory is built in its
module, so nearly every case has an n/tack.mod, usually module n. Every file must end with a
newline; a program whose last line has none belongs in invalid.txtar.
internal/invalid documents the format; go test ./test -run
TestInvalidPackages checks every case, and -update rewrites the expected
sections.

-- clause-mismatch/tack.mod --
module calc
-- clause-mismatch/.target --
lexer
-- clause-mismatch/lexer/a.tck --
package lexer
-- clause-mismatch/lexer/b.tck --
package lex
-- clause-mismatch/lexer/c.tck --
package parser
-- clause-mismatch/expected --
stage: load
---

clause-mismatch/lexer/b.tck:1:9: error: package lex does not match its directory; expected package lexer
01 |	package lex
   |	        ^~~
02 |	

clause-mismatch/lexer/c.tck:1:9: error: package parser does not match its directory; expected package lexer
01 |	package parser
   |	        ^~~~~~
02 |	

